[00:00.00]Proposition one asks whether California should borrow money for
[00:03.80]housing.
[00:05.02]A bond supplies money now, while repayments and interest
[00:08.52]stretch across future budgets.
[00:10.88]Some affordable housing already receives public grants or low-cost
[00:15.10]loans.
[00:16.26]Developers combine that assistance with other financing,
[00:19.68]usually in exchange for keeping homes affordable to eligible
[00:23.18]households.
[00:24.30]This measure authorizes eleven point two five billion
[00:29.14]dollars: ten billion for housing programs,
[00:31.94]and one point two five billion for veterans’ home
[00:34.68]loans.
[00:35.96]Those two portions have different repayment sources.
[00:39.23]Imagine a renter looking for an affordable apartment.
[00:42.83]An apartment project might have land and a builder
[00:45.31]but still need cheaper financing to offer below-market rents.
[00:49.67]A public loan can help close that gap.
[00:52.59]This illustrates the mechanism,
[00:54.55]not a guarantee of a particular rent.
[00:57.27]A housing counselor explains the available programs.
[01:01.00]The largest allocation is seven point two billion dollars
[01:03.78]to acquire, build,
[01:06.10]or renovate multifamily rental housing.
[01:08.98]Projects generally reserve units for lower-income households for fifty-five
[01:12.64]years.
[01:14.26]Other allocations support homeownership, infrastructure,
[01:17.80]farmworker housing, student housing, and tribal housing.
[01:22.06]Eligibility depends on the particular program;
[01:25.20]passing the bond does not give every applicant a
[01:27.52]home.
[01:27.70]The analyst estimates subsidies for up to forty thousand
[01:31.34]multifamily units.
[01:33.20]That includes housing acquired or renovated,
[01:36.40]so it is not a promise of forty thousand
[01:38.78]additional newly built homes.
[01:41.26]The General Fund would repay the housing portion at
[01:43.92]about five hundred to six hundred million dollars a
[01:46.96]year for roughly twenty-five years.
[01:50.10]Veterans’ loan payments would repay their separate bond portion.
[01:53.70]A voter might support financing affordable homes sooner rather
[01:57.92]than waiting for enough money in annual budgets.
[02:01.48]Supporting construction and preserving restricted rents can help specific
[02:05.28]households even when the broader market remains expensive.
[02:09.63]You might ask whether the long commitment is worthwhile.
[02:13.27]A skeptical voter could prefer paying as funds become
[02:16.15]available, or addressing permitting and construction costs first.
[02:21.35]Interest adds expense, and future repayments compete with other
[02:25.11]services.
[02:26.49]Money alone does not remove every obstacle to building.
[02:30.23]In the September PPIC survey of likely voters,
[02:33.71]55 percent said yes,
[02:35.79]43 percent no, and 2 percent were unsure.
[02:39.55]These are dated survey results,
[02:41.61]not votes already cast.
[02:43.53]Yes authorizes this borrowing;
[02:46.19]no rejects this bond, while existing housing programs continue.
